1. Scope and definitions

“Engine” means the original Forge source code, tools, documentation, and
starter assets that are not accompanied by a separate license.
“Copyright Holder” means a person holding rights to the relevant part of
the Engine.
“Product” means a game or other user application using the Forge Core in
whole or in part, in source or compiled form.

For this Engine version, “Core” is exhaustively defined by these files:
  engine/src/main.cpp
  engine/src/config.cpp
  engine/src/log.cpp
  engine/src/runtime.cpp
  engine/include/forge/engine.hpp
  tools/packager.py

Copying, renaming, or moving these files, or transferring their code into
other files, does not change the relevant code's status as Core. Files in
new versions may be designated Core only explicitly in that version's
license. CORE.md explains this boundary but does not expand it.

“Modified Core” means Core whose source code or implementation has been
changed by a Product developer or third party outside an official Forge
release or documented approval by the copyright holders of the affected
code. This is the objective criterion for “non-recommended changes”:
a developer's own opinion that a change is beneficial does not remove
the obligation to identify its modified origin.

Using an unchanged official version, ordinary compilation, changing OS or
architecture, configuring JSON, or modifying only non-Core components
does not by itself constitute a Core modification.

2. Permissions

Subject to this license, the Copyright Holder grants any person a free,
nonexclusive permission to use, study, copy, modify, combine with other
software, and distribute the Engine in source or binary form, including
in commercial and noncommercial Products.

Products and copies of the Engine may be sold. No royalties are required.
This license does not require disclosure of your game's source code,
your own scripts, assets, or independently created additions. You may
choose licenses for independently created code and content. That choice
does not remove the conditions governing included original Forge code.

Core modification is permitted: forks, fixes, and implementation
replacement are not prohibited. Section 3 applies to Modified Core.

3. Required attribution in Products

When providing a Product to other users, publishing it, or demonstrating
it publicly, display explicit Forge attribution:

  • on the loading screen when the Product starts; and
  • in the main menu or an About / About the Engine section accessible
    directly from the Product's menu.

Standard notice:
  “Uses the Forge engine”.

For a Product using Modified Core, the notice in BOTH locations must
identify that modification:
  “Built on the Forge engine (modified core)”.

A translation preserving the exact meaning of these notices is permitted.

Attribution must be readable, have sufficient contrast, and remain
visible long enough for ordinary reading. It may match the Product's
style; a logo is optional. Hidden text, a terminal/log message alone,
or a notice only in documentation, credits, or a store page does not
replace both required locations.

If there is no loading screen, add a startup screen with the notice.
If there is no menu, add a user-accessible information screen through a
menu or a separate Product navigation entry. Debug runs, automated tests,
and internal builds neither provided to other users nor demonstrated
publicly do not require these screens.

Copying a separate non-Core module or asset without using Forge Core
does not by itself make another application a Product under this
license or require engine attribution in its UI. License and copyright
notice preservation requirements still apply.

4. Non-Core modifications

Unless a file or asset explicitly carries a separate license, everything
outside the Core may freely be modified, replaced, extended, removed,
moved, or renamed for your game or application. This includes JSON
settings, scenes, game scripts, modules, objects, materials, textures,
models, audio, shaders, graphics/audio implementations, and physics.

In this version, engine/src/world.cpp (including physics),
engine/src/render.cpp, engine/src/audio.cpp, engine/include/forge/gl.hpp,
graphics/*, and modules/* are outside the Core. Modifying these alone
does not require the “modified core” notice. If Core files or code moved
from them are also modified, the Modified Core rule applies.

Changes to game files require no approval from Engine developers and do
not transfer rights to your independently created game to them.

5. Distribution and preservation of terms

Include the full text of this license and NOTICE in all distributed
Engine copies and Products containing the Engine. Preserve existing
copyright and license notices. These files may reside in a distribution's
licenses directory.

When distributing modified Core sources, describe changes in accompanying
documentation or version history so they can be distinguished from an
official Forge release. Do not present your fork as an unchanged official
release.

Renaming the Engine or placing it under a different license cannot remove
attribution and notice requirements for included original Forge code.
You may impose additional terms on independent contributions if they do
not deprive recipients of rights to original code granted here.

Third-party library, font, and other separately licensed asset licenses
apply independently. This license does not alter their terms or grant
rights beyond those granted by their copyright holders. Information is
provided in THIRD_PARTY.md and alongside those components.

6. Name and absence of endorsement

The Forge name may be used for truthful attribution and origin
identification. This license grants no right to claim Forge copyright
holders endorse your game or fork, and conveys no rights to third-party
trademarks.

